# Hostile audit — THIN rich-cut synchronization and linear weak rail

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Scope:** `target_c_high_t_tb1_thin_rich_cut_phase_sync_t18687_2026-09-17.md` and `target_c_high_t_tb1_thin_linear_weak_rail_t18687_2026-09-17.md`  
**Status:** audit/correction note; C-II remains open

I rechecked the two new THIN deductions against the upstream exact-support, owner-spacing, superdiameter-thrackle, and phase-budget statements.

The main deductions survive. One bookkeeping line in the phase-purification section needs the following asymptotic reading.

## 1. Correction to the phase-purification threshold

The rich-cut theorem proves

\[
F_*\le H_{\rm thin}+o(1),
\qquad
H_{\rm thin}={16C_{\rm phase}\over3\gamma}.
\]

Therefore choose a deterministic sequence

\[
\varepsilon_D\downarrow0
\]

which dominates the positive part of that `o(1)` remainder, so that eventually

\[
F_*\le H_{\rm thin}+\varepsilon_D.
\]

The deletion threshold in TR.30 should be read as

\[
\boxed{
\delta_{*b}>{H_{\rm thin}+\varepsilon_D\over\sigma D}
}
\]

rather than using the fixed `+1` if one wants the exact coefficient displayed in TR.32. Markov then deletes at most `sigma D` columns and every retained column satisfies

\[
\delta_{*b}
\le {H_{\rm thin}\over\sigma D}+o(D^{-1}).
\]

Thus the stated coefficient

\[
K_{\rm thin}=H_{\rm thin}/0.01
\]

is valid. Alternatively one may keep the literal `+1` threshold and replace the displayed constant by `(H_thin+1)/0.01`; none of the density or geometric conclusions changes.

This is a constant-level bookkeeping correction, not a change to the rich-cut extraction.

## 2. Audit of the rich-cut extraction

The potentially delicate step is replacing a vertex-disjoint THIN matching by the full owned THIN family.

For two THIN chords:

- the superdiameter theorem guarantees that they meet;
- if they share a source endpoint, their **closed endpoint-region intervals** share that endpoint region;
- if they do not share an endpoint, they cross properly;
- disjoint closed endpoint-region intervals would place the two hull chords on opposite sides of one selected O--O crosscut and make hull endpoint alternation impossible.

Hence the closed intervals pairwise intersect and one-dimensional Helly gives a common closed region overlap. This use of closed region intervals is essential: the selected-cut intervals themselves need not intersect when two blockers merely share an endpoint region.

The short-overlap counting is also stable. If the common overlap has width `<3N`, fifteen-spacing leaves fewer than `N/5+1` owners inside it. One exterior side has at least `2N/5-O(1)` owners; the farther half of that side supplies `N/5-O(1)` blockers across at least `3N-O(1)` common cuts. The certificate exhausts the finite integer rounding pattern over a wide range of `N`.

The resulting linear rich-cut count is therefore unaffected:

\[
Q_D>
\left({3\gamma\over16}-o(1)\right)D,
\]

with more than

\[
\left({\gamma\over80}-o(1)\right)D
\]

THIN blockers on each rich cut.

## 3. Audit of the weak-rail upgrade

On the selected blocker-rich row, fix the owner shore and one physical owner host. Owner coordinates are distinct, so owner source vertices are distinct.

For two surviving chords `p_sq_s` and `p_tq_t`, with both owners on the same shore:

- they cannot share an owner;
- an owner cannot equal the other chord's partner because the two endpoint shores are disjoint;
- therefore either `q_s=q_t`, or the two chords have four distinct hull endpoints and cross properly.

The complementary-shore crossing-order lemma applies in the second case. Equality in the first case merely inserts a plateau. Thus the partner sequence is weakly ordered, not necessarily strictly ordered. Feeding that weak order through the one-turn partner-region word gives a weakly unimodal region-label sequence, and one monotone half survives.

On the decreasing half, strict owner advance alone makes the support containment strict even if several consecutive chords have the same partner vertex or partner-region label. On the increasing half one obtains the stated staircase.

Accordingly the final density

\[
|R_*|>
\left({\gamma\over640}-o(1)\right)D
\]

is valid, but the word **weak** is important: partner repetition is not eliminated.

## 4. What is and is not proved

The audited THIN normal form is now:

```text
one selected row with O(1) total angular defect;
>0.6436D inverse-D near-60 columns on that row;
linear circumcenter flanks;
linear many inactive superdiameter blockers on the row;
and a linear owner-injective staircase/nested weak rail after constant-factor thinning.
```

No inactive blocker has been promoted to an actual diameter edge, switching edge, or positive-KKT edge. The remaining geometric escape is real: many rail chords may share opposite-shore partner vertices, producing high-degree superdiameter stars. Existing circumradius control bounds such degrees only sublinearly, not by a constant.

**Audit disposition:** rich-cut and weak-rail arguments survive; phase-purification threshold corrected as above; C-II remains open.